// The negative half of "an all-returning `match` counts as returning": the rule is gated on
// EXHAUSTIVENESS, so a `match` the checker cannot prove total still leaves a path to the end of
// the body and E0048 still fires. Believing otherwise would let a function fall off its end —
// far worse than the false positive the rule removes — so the analysis only ever counts a `match`
// the checker's own E0011 judgement calls exhaustive.
//
// `guarded`: every variant has an arm, but `Signal.Go`'s is guarded — the checker cannot prove a
// guard ever true, so `Go` stays uncovered (E0011) and the `match` can fall through (E0048).
// `open`: an `int` scrutinee has an open domain and there is no `_`, so no value is guaranteed to
// enter an arm. E0011 stays silent (open domains are the runtime backstop's job) but E0048 fires.
// expect: error E0048 at 21:4
// expect: error E0011 at 22:5
// expect: error E0048 at 30:4
// expect: exit 1
enum Signal {
    Go;
    Wait;
    Stop
}

fn guarded(s: Signal, hot: bool): int {
    match s {
        Signal.Go if hot => { return 1 },
        Signal.Wait => { return 2 },
        Signal.Stop => { return 3 },
    }
}
echo guarded(Signal.Go, true)

fn open(n: int): int {
    match n {
        1 => { return 1 },
        2 => { return 2 },
    }
}
echo open(1)
